f1a9a6fd21" :question/math-academy-id "q-327871" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} & \\le \\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}+2 & \\le \\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le \\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\le y\\le \\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{5}{4}\\le y\\le \\frac{11}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc55285-7159-598c-b031-86c3a09369ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc55285-7159-598c-b031-86c3a09369ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300452} :answer-field/id #uuid "5454d034-fb34-5c9d-9fdc-5040c6123974" :answer-field/key "fie